description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:481-487:

    /// Borrow the `AiaFetcher` this verifier holds.
    ///
    /// Exposed for diagnostic purposes; callers debugging chain-build
    /// failures can inspect which fetcher is wired in.
    pub fn aia(&self) -> &A {
        self.aia
    }

The docstring describes the use case ("diagnostic") but does not document the contract. Open questions a caller would ask:

1. **Is the returned reference stable across verify_one calls?** Today, yes — the Verifier holds a &'a A and the borrow just hands it through. But the caller cannot tell from the contract.

2. **Does mutation through interior-mutability on A affect subsequent verify_one calls?** Today, yes — verify_one captures `self.aia` (a copy of the &'a A pointer) and calls `batch_fetch` on it during the slow path. An AiaFetcher with `Cell<Counter>` for hit counts will reflect through Verifier::aia(). The caller cannot tell from the contract whether this is supported or implementation-detail.

3. **Why is there only `aia()` and not `revocation()`, `signature_verifier()`, `anchors()`, `policy()`?** The Verifier has five private fields; only one is exposed. The selection is arbitrary from the API perspective. A future caller wanting to diagnose "which RevocationChecker is wired in" has the same need but no method to call. They will file an issue asking for symmetric accessors, OR pkix-chain will grow `revocation()`, `signature_verifier()`, etc. — and at that point each one is a forever commitment.

WHY IT MATTERS:

For a debugging-only accessor, the contract gaps are mostly harmless (callers debug, they don't depend). But the inclusion of `aia()` while OTHER fields stay private is itself an API decision. Either:

(a) Drop `aia()`. Make the debugging facility a `Debug` impl on `Verifier` (printing the type name of A; it's all the diagnostic info you can get from a generic without further trait bounds). The current `aia()` is essentially this with a friendlier API.

(b) Symmetrize. Add `revocation() -> &R`, `signature_verifier() -> &V`, `anchors() -> &[TrustAnchor]`, `policy() -> &ValidationPolicy`. Now the Verifier is a transparent handle to its components, and the contract is "you can re-read what you put in." Documented as such.

(c) Keep just `aia()` but document why it's special (e.g. AIA is the only seam likely to need runtime inspection because it's the only one with built-in choices like `NoAiaFetcher`).

The current shape is option (a) but spelled as a method instead of a Debug impl. The minor naming/discoverability gap is the underspecification: a contributor reading lib.rs has no way to know whether `aia()` is "the start of a getter family" or "a one-off because A is special." A future PR adding `revocation()` becomes a coin-flip decision.

LOW PRIORITY. This is the smallest of the immutability/underspecification issues — the wrong choice doesn't break callers, it just adds slight API noise. Filing for completeness; flag with the opinion label if you'd prefer not to track it.
